<div class="image-gallery"><figure><img src="/assets/images/landmarks/first-national-bank-building/240-1882-first-natl-bank-three-men.jpg" alt="Early First National Bank of Mandan"></figure></div>

<p>In 1880, Mandan was a thriving village. {% person "michael-lang" %}Michael Lang{% endperson %}, {% person "james-bellows" %}James Bellows{% endperson %}, and {% person "hiram-r-lyons" %}Hiram R. Lyons{% endperson %} reportedly recognized the need for a bank while walking through town, sat on a pile of rock, and organized one. The First National Bank of Mandan was established on November 22, 1881. Its frame building stood where the three men had planned the bank, on Main Street near Front Street and Stark Avenue, now Collins Avenue. The bank owned the adjacent corner building, occupied by Taylor Drug Company.</p>

<p>The bank's first officers were {% person "c-edgar-haupt" %}C. Edgar Haupt{% endperson %}, president; James Bellows, vice president; and Hiram R. Lyons, cashier. Directors included Michael Lang, {% person "le-reed" %}Le Reed{% endperson %}, Haupt, {% person "lovet-gill" %}Lovet Gill{% endperson %}, Bellows, and {% person "robert-hagar" %}Robert Hagar{% endperson %}. Lang became vice president in 1884.</p>

<p>By 1906, deposits had increased twelvefold and the bank needed more space. The original frame buildings were moved, and the present two-story brick building was constructed across both sites at Main Street and Collins Avenue.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure><img src="/assets/images/landmarks/first-national-bank-building/334-first-national-bank-photo.jpg" alt="First National Bank building around 1908"><figcaption>First National Bank building, c. 1908</figcaption></figure>
  <figure><img src="/assets/images/landmarks/first-national-bank-building/334-fnbm-1949-main-st-lalonde.jpg" alt="First National Bank on Main Street in 1949"><figcaption>Main Street, 1949</figcaption></figure>
</div>

<p>The building underwent three major expansions or remodelings. A two-story, 46-by-12-foot rear addition was completed in 1911. Rapid commercial growth prompted a matching two-story north addition in 1927, increasing total floor space by approximately 60 percent. In 1948, the exterior was remodeled in the Greek Revival style. Arched openings were squared, an ashlar facade covered the arches, and a projecting pavilion and fretwork unified the exterior. "First National Bank" was engraved above the main entrance.</p>

<div class="image-gallery"><figure><img src="/assets/images/landmarks/first-national-bank-building/250-first-natl-bank-10-dollars-1921.jpg" alt="Ten-dollar note issued by the First National Bank of Mandan in 1921"></figure></div>

<p>As was common during the 1920s, the bank issued its own paper currency. Counterfeiting and the need for tighter control over the money supply eventually ended the practice.</p>

<div class="image-gallery"><figure><img src="/assets/images/landmarks/first-national-bank-building/300-fnbm-2010.jpg" alt="Wells Fargo bank in Mandan in 2010"><figcaption>Wells Fargo, Mandan, 2010</figcaption></figure></div>

<p>In September 1963, the bank moved to a new $350,000 building at 111 North Second Avenue NW, formerly the site of Remund Ford-Mercury. Meisner and Company of Bismarck was general contractor, with Sanitary Plumbing and Heating and Power Electric of Mandan as mechanical subcontractors. {% person "robert-m-leslie" %}Robert M. Leslie{% endperson %} was bank president.</p>

<p>The bank eventually joined Northwest Bancorporation, which became part of Wells Fargo. The original building is a contributing property in the Mandan Commercial Historic District, listed in 1985.</p>

<div class="image-gallery"><figure><img src="/assets/images/landmarks/first-national-bank-building/708-fnbm-2010-east-streetview.jpg" alt="Original First National Bank building in 2010"><figcaption>Original First National Bank building, 2010</figcaption></figure></div>

<p>The building continues to house commercial services, and its engraved "First National Bank" sign remains in place.</p>
